          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP682 ParkVista View (FOUND)

Exploring FCP caches today and found ourselves too close to this cap to ignore. In good shape, hiding under a few leaves.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="704099516">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-07-22T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1685537">nosweat6060</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP682 ParkVista View [FOUND]

I began this morning with a plan to look for 4 caps In Fish Creek Provincial Park west of Macleod Trail and then look for another 4 in the park but east of Macleod Trail. This all unfolded according to plan. I still had a little time on my hands so decided to look for another pair in SE Calgary. I ended the day with a search for a geocache near the last cap. Thanks to ofth and vdD for setting up and maintaining these activities. I made a note of all the numbers and will add a few photos when I am near my photo software. This was brass cap #8 for today.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="699253364">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="50.913450" lon="-114.031083" />
          <groundspeak:date>2017-07-05T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1074559">van der Decken</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP682 - ParkVista View

Elev. 1041 metres&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I'm glad that I waited a day to come down to Fish Creek Park. It was a whole lot calmer. That made for much easier pedalling. And as it was going to be another long day on the bike, the easier the pedalling was, the less my knees (and other assorted body parts!) would be upset with me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There was nothing to mark this one in a sea of crunchy yellow grass. There were little divots here and there, but they seemed to be of the rodent variety rather than the surveying kind. I could have paced off the distance from the curb, but that seemed like more work than I felt like on a balmy summer day. So I just followed the GPSr until the coordinates matched up, looked down, and there was the cap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That &lt;em&gt;never&lt;/em&gt; happens.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The ground level cap is in quite reasonable shape for an urban cap. It has a few tool marks on it, but only a few. All the numbers are easily read.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the cap, outforthehunt. You know, it &lt;em&gt;still&lt;/em&gt; feels weird not to be sending you the pictures!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(FOUND)&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
